Important Thread Components :
Thread class:To create and manage threads.
Runnable interface:To provide the task for the thread.
run() method:Defines what the thread will execute.
start() method:Starts a new thread and calls run() internally.
sleep():Temporarily pauses thread.
join():One thread waits for another to finish.
yield():Thread gives chance to other threads.
synchronized:Used to avoid race conditions.
wait(), notify(), notifyAll():Used for thread communication.

| Feature                 | notify()                    | notifyAll()                               |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Wakes up                | Only **one** waiting thread | **All** waiting threads                   |
| Selection               | JVM randomly chooses        | All waiting threads are moved to Runnable |
| Risk of missing wake-up | High                        | Low                                       |
| Risk of deadlock        | Higher                      | Lower                                     |
| Performance             | Better (less overhead)      | More overhead                             |


| Feature        | `join()`                                       | `yield()`                        |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Purpose        | Make one thread wait for another               | Give chance to other threads     |
| Control        | Strong control                                 | Weak suggestion (JVM may ignore) |
| Blocks Thread? | ✔ Yes                                          | ❌ No                           |
| Used For       | Ordering tasks, dependencies                   | Avoiding CPU hogging             |
| Behavior       | Current thread pauses until target thread ends | Scheduler may switch threads     |

What is Race Condition? 
A race condition happens when:
Two or more threads->Access shared data at the same time->And the final result depends on who runs first
If threads “race” to update the same value → unpredictable output.

How to Fix a Race Condition?
1.Use synchronized block or method
synchronized(this) {
    count++;
}
2. Use Atomic Variables : Thread-safe and no locking needed
AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
count.incrementAndGet();
3. Use Lock (ReentrantLock)
lock.lock();
try {
    count++;
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
4. Avoid shared mutable state
Design threads to use local copies or immutable data.

Starvation:
A thread never gets a chance to run
Because other threads keep taking the CPU or lock
The starving thread is ALIVE, but never scheduled

Livelock:
Threads are RUNNING but making NO progress
They keep reacting to each other
They are “alive” but “stuck”
Avoid Fairlock:
Lock lock = new ReentrantLock(true); // fair lock

Avoid Deadlock:
Use consistent lock ordering:
