Producer-Consumer example:
class SharedBuffer {
    private int item;
    private boolean hasItem = false;   // buffer empty initially

    public synchronized void produce(int value) throws InterruptedException {
        while (hasItem) {          // buffer is full
            wait();                // release lock & wait
        }
        item = value;
        System.out.println("Produced: " + item);
        hasItem = true;
        notify();                  // wake consumer
    }

    public synchronized void consume() throws InterruptedException {
        while (!hasItem) {         // buffer empty
            wait();                // release lock & wait
        }
        System.out.println("Consumed: " + item);
        hasItem = false;
        notify();                  // wake producer
    }
}

}

public class ProducerConsumerExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        SharedBuffer buffer = new SharedBuffer();

        Thread producer = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                try { buffer.produce(i); }
                catch (Exception e) { }
            }
        });

        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                try { buffer.consume(); }
                catch (Exception e) { }
            }
        });

        producer.start();
        consumer.start();
    }
}


Synchronized means only one thread executes a block/method at a time (safe but slow).
Asynchronous means multiple threads run independently without waiting (fast but unsafe unless controlled).

